## Context

The two methods to create and maintain the network load balancer service required for ingressing the Go traffic and the kube API traffic from the two production clusters on GoB. These two methods are:

1) use a dedicated, separate, new Kubernetes cluster (i.e. GoKube) created by the kubernetes team specifically for the purpose of being the Go Ingress point, as [it is for Kubernetes](https://github.com/kubernetes/kube-on-go.gds.gov.uk); and
2) manage the existing kube-system Kubernetes cluster on GoB to be the Go Ingress point, by way of using kops `kops ingress set-ingress-controller [kops-cluster-name]` (i.e. for GoKube at kops-cluster-name-go-ingress-ctrl) for the set of Kubernetes clusters on GoB that are configured to [be the Go Ingress point](https://github.com/alphagov/gds-cluster.git) on the following basis:
   - go-g4, go-g5 and go-g6 have no (external) Ingress nodes and no kops Ingress controller deployed in them;
   - use an internal cluster, called go-kops-cluster-name, which has a single kops-ingress-controller node which is deployed with access to the Kubernetes API and creates SSL certificates; and
   - use an external cluster, called go-kops-cluster-name-g6, which has an internal and external Ingress node with a single kops-ingress-controller node which is deployed with access to the Kubernetes API and creates SSL certificates;
   - the go-g4 and go-g5 clusters are configured to [have the [external Ingress node](https://github.com/alphagov/gds-cluster.git) set up to route SSL traffic from the `kops-cluster-name-g6` external Ingress node directly to the kube-system Ingress controller](https://github.com/alphagov/gds-cluster.git);
   - the go-g6 cluster [has the [kops-cluster-name-g6 external Ingress node](https://github.com/alphagov/gds-cluster.git) configured to route traffic to the internal and external Ingress node, and the internal Ingress node routes directly to the kube-system Ingress controller](https://github.com/alphagov/gds-cluster.git);

## Decision

We will adopt the second method of managing the existing kube-system Kubernetes cluster on GoB as the single Go Ingress point, and don't bother creating a separate Go Kube cluster, because:

1) the [Go Kube cluster is non-trivial to set up](https://github.com/kops/kops/blob/master/docs/quick-reference.md);
2) the [Go Kube config tools](https://github.com/kops/kops/blob/master/docs/config-management.md) (i.e. `kops config-set`, `kops config-set --set .`, `kops cluster-set`, `kops cluster-set --set .`, `kops ingress set-ingress-controller`, `kops ingress set-ingress-controller [kops-cluster-name]`, etc. etc.) are not trivial to use;
3) the [Go Kube ingress controller](https://gops.io/kops/ingress/index.html) (i.e. `kops ingress set-ingress-controller [kops-cluster-name]`) has [no support for [Ingress controller URL](https://docs.cloudfoundry.org/devguide/ingress.html#Ingress-controller-URL)](https://docs.cloudfoundry.org/devguide/ingress.html#Ingress-controller-URL) and [does not [support setting the [Ingress controller [IP address](https://github.com/alphagov/gds-cluster.git#ingress-controller-ip-address-and-domain)](https://github.com/alphagov/gds-cluster.git#ingress-controller-ip-address-and-domain)](https://github.com/alphagov/gds-cluster.git#ingress-controller-ip-address-and-domain);
4) [Ingress controller URLs are not [useful](https://docs.cloudfoundry.org/devguide/ingress.html#Ingress

