## URL Schema

### Context
A URL schema names a resource type and describes how its url should be structured.

### Decision
We use an optional schema to be defined for each resource type. The schema is then used to validate against the URL that is attempted to be saved to a collection. 

### Sample
#### schema.json
```json
{
  "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-06/schema#",
  "$id": "http://example.com/my-awesome-resource",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "required-property-1": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true,
      "description": "This is a required string property"
    },
    "required-property-2": {
      "type": "integer",
      "required": true,
      "description": "This is a required integer property"
    },
    "optional-property": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "This is an optional string property"
    }
  },
  "description": "This is a description of the resource schema"
}
```

#### Schema example usage
```js
const validate = require('validate.js');
const schema = require('./schema.json');
const data = {
  "required-property-1": "my-property-value",
  "required-property-2": 42,
  "optional-property": "my-optional-property-value"
};

const validateData = validate(data, schema);
if (validateData !== undefined && Object.keys(validateData).length > 0) {
  console.log(validateData);
} else {
  console.log('Data is valid according to the schema');
}
```

